  ],
  "definition": "A predetermined order that automatically closes a trade at a target price to lock in profits without manual intervention. Targets are anchored to market structure—swing highs/lows, liquidity pools, or fair value gaps—and are often tiered (TP1, TP2, TP3) using Fibonacci levels. The structural swing point broken to trigger the setup serves as the primary, high-probability target, while extension levels and trailing stops capture extended trend moves at a favorable risk-to-reward ratio.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Indicator-Supplement": "An order that automatically closes a trade at a predetermined price to secure profits. Targets are typically placed at key market structure levels such as swing points (swing highs/lows) or liquidity pools, and may be combined with a trailing stop to capture extended trend moves.",
    "SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125": "A predetermined tiered exit price using Fibonacci levels. TP1 targets the previous swing high (longs) or swing low (shorts)—the structural level broken to trigger the retracement setup—as a near-guaranteed conservative target where double tops/bottoms form. TP2/TP3 are Fibonacci extensions (e.g., -0.382, -0.5, -0.618) projected beyond the swing extreme, used with partial profit-taking or trailing stops."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Set the initial/primary take-profit (TP1) at the nearest opposing swing point — swing high for longs, swing low for shorts. PLv-X125 specifies this is the structural level broken to confirm the Break of Structure, corresponding to the 0.0 level of the Fibonacci retracement tool.",
    "Identify upcoming liquidity pools (sellside for shorts, buyside for longs) as potential take-profit levels (SMC-Indicator-Supplement).",
    "Consider the most recent fair value gap as a take-profit target if it lies within a reasonable distance (SMC-Indicator-Supplement).",
    "Set TP2 at a Fibonacci extension projected beyond the original swing extreme (e.g., -0.382 or -0.5 extension) (PLv-X125).",
    "Set TP3 at a further Fibonacci extension (e.g., -0.618 or -1.0, representing one full standard deviation of the impulse move) (PLv-X125).",
    "In extreme momentum or swing trade scenarios, use the -1.618 Fibonacci extension as an additional take-profit target (PLv-X125).",
    "Draw Fibonacci extensions from the same impulse move used for the retracement — measured bottom to top (longs) or top to bottom (shorts), including wicks (PLv-X125).",
    "Verify the distance from entry (0.5 or 0.618 retracement) to the target yields at least a 1:1 risk-to-reward ratio relative to the stop loss (PLv-X125).",
    "Before targeting extended levels, look left on the chart to verify whether historical price targets or support/resistance levels exist near the projected take-profit zones (PLv-X125).",
    "When using a trailing approach, move the stop-loss behind key structural levels to lock in profits as the trade progresses (both sources).",
    "Place the take-profit order at the time of entry alongside the stop loss to create a set-it-and-forget-it position (PLv-X125).",
    "Place take-profit levels at a distance from entry that avoids prolonged exposure and unnecessary risk (SMC-Indicator-Supplement).",
    "Do not extend the target beyond the previous swing extreme without momentum confirmation; assume a double top or double bottom may form at TP1 (PLv-X125)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Setting the take-profit too far from entry (e.g., targeting extended Fibonacci extensions instead of the safer previous swing high/low as the primary target), leading to prolonged exposure, potential reversals, and reduced consistency/win rate.",
    "Placing take-profit at arbitrary levels without considering market structure or key reaction zones.",
    "Holding the trade expecting further continuation instead of exiting at the predetermined conservative target.",
    "Not placing the take-profit order in advance, leading to emotional exits or missed profits.",
    "Treating Fibonacci levels as exact prices rather than zones, causing missed exits when price reverses slightly before or after the precise level.",
    "Not using a trailing stop for TP2 and TP3, risking giving back unrealized profits after TP1 is hit.",
    "Expecting all three TP levels to be hit on every trade instead of recognizing TP1 is near-guaranteed while TP2/TP3 are conditional on momentum.",
    "Entering take-profit targets blindly without assessing the momentum of the move to gauge how far price is likely to extend.",
    "Targeting the -1.618 extension on short-term or low-momentum trades when it suits only swing trades with extreme momentum.",
    "Failing to look left on the chart to check for historical price targets or support/resistance near projected take-profit zones.",
    "Drawing the Fibonacci tool before a retracement actually begins, misidentifying the correct previous high/low.",
    "Applying take-profit logic without first confirming a candle close beyond the previous structure level (Break of Structure).",
    "Placing take-profit targets during low-volume sessions or news events where price ignores structure levels.",
    "Setting entry at the 0.5 Fibonacci level instead of the 0.618, which compresses the risk-to-reward ratio."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Take-profit orders automatically secure gains, reducing emotional interference and enforcing a favorable risk-to-reward ratio. Both projects anchor targets to market structure rather than arbitrary levels. SMC-Indicator-Supplement places targets at swing points, liquidity pools, or fair value gaps, combining them with trailing stops to capture extended moves. SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125 uses a tiered Fibonacci approach: TP1 at the broken previous swing high/low is a high-probability conservative exit (price has already proven it can reach it and often forms double tops/bottoms there), so close the majority of the position (e.g., 80%) at TP1, then trail stops for remaining portions toward TP2/TP3. Targeting only TP1 yields roughly 1:1 to 1:2 risk-to-reward and suits scalping on lower timeframes; extended extensions require momentum confirmation.",
  "merge_notes": "Both sources agree on structure-based targeting and trailing stops, but differ in framework. SMC-Indicator-Supplement is broader, using swing points, liquidity pools, and fair value gaps. SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125 is more prescriptive, formalizing a tiered TP1/TP2/TP3 system built on Fibonacci retracement/extension levels, partial profit-taking percentages, Break-of-Structure confirmation, and momentum-gating for extended targets—detail not present in the first source.",
